hi there steve coffin um i want to  follow up my previous video which  talked about how the most important  thing in language learning was to have  fun or to enjoy doing it  and that was whatever was enjoyable was  effective now  i must admit that in some cases that's  that's not always the case but it's it's  a good rule of thumb it works a lot of  the time  related to that is the idea that in  language learning i always try to do the  things that are the least amount of work  i like to do uh effortless language  learning and i borrow the word  effortless from two sources one is aj  hogue  h-o-g-u-e who is a great  teacher of english via the internet um  hello uh aj if you ever have a chance to  see this video a great man and his uh  channel and his website is called  effortless english but  when i wrote my other source is the uh  is taoist philosophy or taoist  philosophy when i wrote my book  the linguist the personal guide to  language learning about gosh it's got to  be eight or nine years ago  um i began it with what i call the  parable of the crooked tree  because drunza who is a a philosopher a  taoist philosopher  whom i refer to in my book and his basic  principle in life was to just follow  what was natural what was effortless  and don't try and force things  and typically the taoist philosophy was  in opposition to confucius which had a  whole bunch of rules of what you should  do and shouldn't do  in order to be a great person  and drunza had this parable of the  crooked tree where his friend says you  know  this tree is crooked and his lumber is  not good for anything and your  philosophy is not good for anything  either  and uh drunks are replied well yeah  that's because you have to make use of  it for what its nature is so  you know a crooked tree you can sit  underneath it in the shade  in my i'm in the lumber business you can  sometimes some of those gnarly old uh  trees produce very decorative lumber  that's very expensive anyway taoism is  also about effortless  wu way in other words no effort you're  not fighting it and i believe you  learned better in that way so i'm going  to talk about all the things that i do  that are easy and effortless and  therefore in my opinion  more effective and i made some notes  here  so the first thing is  um you know normally if i'm reading in a  language where i read well enough to  read away from the computer  i don't look up the words it's too much  trouble  uh on the other hand if i can't read  well enough  it's too much trouble to look words up  in a dictionary because the minute you  close the dictionary you forget the  meaning  and so therefore i read on a computer  where i have an online dictionary and  where once i've looked the word up you  know as in link it's then highlighted so  the next time i see it the next time i  see it in any subsequent material i can  see that i've looked it up before i can  see the meaning and so forth so i'm not  you know just looking words up in a  dictionary and then  having to forget them  okay another thing that's effortless  when you read grammar and i believe we  should read grammar rules it's kind of  helps give us a sense of the language  don't try to remember anything don't try  to learn anything don't try to  understand anything  just treat it as a a spark an exposure  of something that might help you  eventually get a sense of the language  so don't worry about grammar  don't do questions if you're going  through and i as i mentioned i did find  an old  uh teach yourself check here  that i had bought  many many years ago and i thought i  would try and learn check and i found it  kind of useful i go through it  it has  questions you know grammar drills i  never never do them  however what is very useful is to go to  the back of the book and at the back of  the book you'll find the answers so  rather than  asking you know having to try to answer  the question racking your brain to come  up with the right answer just read all  the answers because then you'll get  concentrated examples  of whatever they're trying to test you  on whether it be the dative case or  pronouns or anything else so i find it  useful more than once to read the  answers to these questions again it's  effortless i don't like doing the  questions because it's too much work  by the same token  when i read something i don't like  answering comprehension questions i  would rather  have misunderstood the text have my own  interpretive interpretation of the text  than have to answer somebody's question  where i have to think about you know why  did this and that and so forth and i  have mentioned before the great  brazilian  educator who said that nothing destroys  the pleasure of reading as much as  being asked questions about what you  have read or being asked to analyze what  you have read so i avoid all of that i  mentioned the other day that russian  textbook uh on history which which was a  wonderful textbook where they had 20  sort of pre-reading questions and i  don't know how many post-reading  questions all of that is effort i don't  do it it spoils the pleasure of reading  and or listening  uh flash cards again i've now finally  resolved i didn't use flash cards very  much but i do find them a break they're  easy to do and as long as they can be  made as easy as possible so i don't have  the flash card with say the check word  on the front and then the answer on the  back i put everything on the front so  though for czech word  in our system we have the english  translation and the captured phrase i  just look at the front and i go through  them very quickly i don't have to think  i'm just being exposed to them there  again effortless another one is this  gold list that i mentioned previously  i'm i'm not convinced that it's  effortless but there are elements of it  that are effortless because you only  write the words down you write them down  and you leave it for 14 days you don't  try and rack your brain you don't try  and your brain you don't have to try to  go over it again and again forcing your  brain to try to learn something that it  isn't going to learn the brain will  learn naturally with enough exposure  it'll learn some things naturally and  it'll learn all things on its own  schedule and you can't force it yeah  here's another thing too i mean you  sometimes get the purists that tell you  that you must only use a monolingual  dictionary i never use a monolingual  dictionary it's much easier to use a  bilingual dictionary  if i'm starting out in a language and  you know i have no words and they're  going to rather than you know book  whatever  you know  knee-high in if i don't know  uh many words in check a monolingual  dictionary is useless but even when i'm  quite advanced i just find that a  bilingual dictionary here's a word bingo  i get this  beginning of a hint of what that word  might mean and i know that only through  a lot of exposure will i eventually get  the hang of that word but i don't want  to spend my time trying to figure out  what this check to check dictionary  definition of that word is i find it  easier to use a bilingual dictionary it  to me is more effortless and whatever's  effortless to me is good  uh yeah the other thing too is  don't worry about things that you don't  understand and don't worry about  you know elements of the new language  that are  basically very strange to you so  one of the things i find  especially when i uh in asian languages  but not only there's a tendency to  introduce the names of every possible  relative early on and in english english  actually is quite poor in terms of  defining family relationships but other  languages have many more words to denote  your  cousin brother  your wife's  mother your uh you know  father's father i don't know a third  cousin  older brother younger sister whatever  it's in korean it's in czech and so  i can't can't be bothered with it those  are concepts we don't have in english  it's very difficult for me to understand  it doesn't really matter  uh you know him her it's good enough  similarly with some of these things that  you know if you're in a language where  they don't have articles like russian or  japanese you're gonna find articles  difficult  uh in english don't worry about it it's  gonna take a long time before those  things sink in  conversely if i'm you know i still don't  understand all the explanations about  the aspects of verbs in russian but i  kind of naturally get it more  uh some of the time some of the time i  don't  i'm aware that this  thing exists  but i've read the explanations nah don't  worry about things that are so very  different similarly in in japanese don't  worry about polite language at first  because it takes a lot of exposure  in order to to have a sense for that so  all i'm saying is when you're learning  it's it's kind of i do believe in this  sort of taoist approach do what's easy  do what's effortless don't answer  questions don't force yourself to learn  things don't cram things into your brain  just do it easily exposure  to the language trust the brain  and  i think you'll end up learning at least  as well so there you have it effortless  language learning thank you for  listening
 

